WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.210
SPEAKER: In our last episode,
we covered ingesting, routing,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.150
storing, and viewing logs from
your services in Cloud Logging.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.430
But what else can you
do with all that data?

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.020
Today, I'm going
to show you how you

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.420
can use Cloud Logging to
further improve your ability

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.210
to keep your services reliable
with features like alerting

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.540
in logs, logs-based metrics,
and capturing application

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.370
exceptions in error reporting.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.310
Welcome to "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.535
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:29.300
Let's say you are running
a production environment

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.340
and you need to know if
any modifications are

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.160
made to the IAM configuration
for this project.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.390
These changes will
show up in audit logs,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:37.820
but you don't
really want to sift

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.520
through those to figure out when
something like this happens.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.740
You could set up a
rudimentary log processing

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.200
pipeline with Cloud Functions,
but now there's an easier way.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.830
Let's have a look at how to
use the new log-based alerts

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.430
in Cloud Logging.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:55.160
From the dashboard, go
to the Products menu.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.270
And from Logging,
select Logs Explorer.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.690
We want to find all logs that
show changes in IAM policies.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.834
That's easy enough to do
with using recent queries.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.570
Once we have the logs
we want to alert on,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:14.790
we can use the Actions
button to create a log alert.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:19.020
Let's name our alert
and add any information

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.640
we want to send with the alert.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.730
Next, let's confirm
our logs query.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.580
Then, let's set the minimum
time between notifications

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.970
to be as frequent as we'd like.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.370
Finally, let's specify
the notification channel

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.630
and save the alert.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.470
The alerting policy
has been created,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.520
and we are ready to test it.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.810
If an IAM policy change is
made, we can go back to logging

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:47.990
and rerun the query to see that
more entries matching our query

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.220
are available.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:54.140
From there, we can go to
Monitoring and select Alerting.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.630
There's a current incident
based on the alerting policy

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:57.740
we created.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.390
We can select the
incident to confirm

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.570
that we have a log query match
that triggered our condition.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.150
We can click View Logs to
see the logs that generated

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.500
the match and the alert.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.199
So that's how you can use
log-based alert policies

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:11.060
to be notified when,
for example, there's

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:15.260
a change in IAM configuration
in your project.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.552
Now, alerting in logs is great.

00:02:16.552 --> 00:02:19.177
But what if you want to know how
many times something like this

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:19.870
happens?

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.940
Or what if the log
payloads have data in them

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.130
that you'd like to
measure over time?

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.710
This is where logs-based
metrics come in.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.240
You can configure
a counter metric

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.160
to measure the permanent
rate of log entries

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.650
that match a query or
a distribution metric

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.163
to capture numeric values
from login to payloads.

00:02:35.163 --> 00:02:36.580
Let's have a look
at how to set up

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.110
a counter metric
in Cloud Logging

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.220
and use it in Cloud Monitoring.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.140
Let's create a metric
to count requests

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.090
to our service running
on App Engine labeled

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.620
by response code.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:52.710
Let's go to the Logs Explorer
and select our Google App

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.700
Engine application.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.190
Let's select an entry that
shows an App Engine request

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.745
and use Show Matching Entries
to get all the logs that

00:03:00.745 --> 00:03:02.120
look like what we
want to include

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.970
as a source for our metric.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.580
We'll again use
the Actions button,

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.840
but this time we'll
create a metric.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.580
We'll select Counter
as the metric type

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.465
and name our metric.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.060
Next, we'll confirm the filter
and add a label to our metric

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.475
that will allow us to separate
request counts by status.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.820
Finally, let's save the metric.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.570
Now, when we go to
log-based metrics,

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:37.950
we can see our metric here.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.980
Once a metric is created
and gathering data,

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.950
we can use it like any
other monitoring metric.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.480
We can view it in
Metrics Explorer,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.610
we can use it for
charts and dashboards,

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.220
and we can even create alerts
using it in a condition.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.830
So that's how you can use
a counter log-based metric

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.320
to count App Engine requests
labeled by response code

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.400
status.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.560
We've now seen how
to get alerts on logs

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.755
and how to use them
to create metrics.

00:04:04.755 --> 00:04:06.130
The last thing I
want to show you

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:09.438
is how to use error reporting
to count, analyze, and aggregate

00:04:09.438 --> 00:04:11.230
the crashes in your
running Cloud Services.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.630
Let's have a look.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:18.370
From the Products menu,
go to Error Reporting.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.100
The initial screen shows you
a summary of all the errors

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.530
that have been seen in your
project over the selected time

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.250
interval.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:28.130
You can click on an error to
get more information about it.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:29.920
The histogram will
show you how frequently

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.470
this error has occurred.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:35.710
Further down, you can see the
parsed and raw stack traces

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:38.640
and the specific
samples of the error.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.520
You can even view the
logs that generated

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.580
that particular sample.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.110
By viewing the logs
for the sample,

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:47.710
you can see information about
where the error originated.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:50.320
Here we can see the
GKE cluster, namespace,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.930
and container that threw
this particular exception.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:54.850
So error reporting
is a great way

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.010
to see application
exceptions in your services

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.110
and quickly figure out
when they're happening

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.180
and where they're coming from.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.310
Services running on
App Engine or Cloud Run

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:06.800
send errors to error
reporting automatically.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.600
You can send errors
from other applications

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.490
to error reporting
by logging into Cloud

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.070
Logging in a format
recognizable by error reporting,

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:16.437
or by using the error
reporting API directly.

00:05:16.437 --> 00:05:18.020
Refer to the
documentation for details

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.190
on both of those options.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.600
Thanks for joining me today.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.450
We covered alerting
on log entries,

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:25.940
using logs-based metrics
to count and aggregate log

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.730
entries, and using
error reporting

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.660
to track application crashes.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.360
I hope these features help you
find issues faster and keep

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.880
your services reliable
and your users happy.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.253
Thanks so much for watching.

00:05:36.253 --> 00:05:38.420
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.120
on more "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:41.990
See you soon.

